---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Wordmark from '../components/Wordmark.astro';
import {
  renderTargetTracker,
  renderCustodyEthnicityCompanion,
} from '../lib/charts.mjs';

const tracker = renderTargetTracker();
const companion = renderCustodyEthnicityCompanion();

const yjsUrl =
  'https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/youth-justice-statistics-2024-to-2025';
const yjsCitation =
  'Youth Justice Statistics 2024 to 2025, Ministry of Justice and Youth Justice Board, January 2026';
const metricUrl =
  'https://github.com/SGilr/prism-r/blob/main/docs/target-metric.md';
const whitePaperUrl =
  'https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a06eb37c0cc74b4523e4e8c/youth-justice-white-paper.pdf';

const headline = [
  { value: '28%', label: 'Black children as a share of children remanded' },
  { value: '18%', label: 'Mixed Heritage children as a share of children remanded' },
  { value: '16%', label: 'Asian children and children in the Other category, as a share of children remanded' },
  { value: '62%', label: 'Remanded children who did not receive a custodial sentence' },
];
---

<Base title="PRISM-R: Remand Disparity Intelligence">
  <article>
    <header class="masthead pb-8">
      <h1 class="reveal text-5xl leading-none sm:text-6xl">
        <Wordmark />
      </h1>
      <p class="strapline reveal reveal-2 mt-4">
        Prevention Risk Intelligence &amp; Stratified Mapping: Remand
      </p>
      <p class="reveal reveal-3 mt-4 text-lg leading-snug text-neutral-600">
        An open analysis of youth remand disproportionality in England and
        Wales
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        PRISM-R examines the over-representation of Black, Mixed Heritage and
        minority-ethnic children among those remanded in the youth justice
        system of England and Wales. It treats that over-representation as a
        prevention question: a pattern that is set well before a child reaches
        a courtroom, and that is visible in earlier decisions, in policing and
        in the circumstances children grow up in.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tool follows children through the decisions that lead to remand and
        shows where disparity enters and where it compounds. It reports
        co-occurrence, not causation, and it draws only on publicly available
        data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 border-y border-neutral-200 py-7">
      <p class="font-sans text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-neutral-500">
        The headline picture
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-2 gap-y-8 lg:grid-cols-4">
        {
          headline.map((item, i) => (
            <div
              class:list={[
                'px-5 first:pl-0',
                i > 0 && 'border-l border-neutral-200',
                i === 2 && 'max-lg:border-l-0 max-lg:pl-0',
              ]}
            >
              <dd class="stat-figure">{item.value}</dd>
              <dt class="mt-2 font-sans text-xs leading-snug text-neutral-600">
                {item.label}
              </dt>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-7 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed text-neutral-500">
        Published statistics, cited as reported in
        <a
          href={yjsUrl}
          class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent"
        >
          {yjsCitation}</a>. The charts on the national picture page are
        PRISM-R's own analysis and are labelled as such.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 border-l-2 border-accent bg-[#f6f5f0] p-5 sm:p-6">
      <h2 class="strapline" style="color: #5f5e5a;">
        Tracking the 25% target
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The Youth Justice White Paper
        <a
          href={whitePaperUrl}
          class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2"
        >
          Cutting Youth Crime. Changing Young Lives.</a>, published on 18 May
        2026, commits to reducing custodial remand for children by 25% over
        this Parliament. The chart shows the youth estate remand population as
        a 12-month rolling average, monthly from 2015, against the baseline
        (the twelve months to March 2025) and the 25% target line. The faint
        line is the unaveraged monthly figure.
      </p>
      <div class="chart mt-5" set:html={tracker.svg} />
      <p class="chart-download"><a href="/data/csv/remand-target-tracker.csv" download>Download this chart's data (CSV)</a></p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed font-semibold">
        {tracker.position}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        {tracker.preCommitment}
      </p>
      <h3 class="mt-6 font-sans text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-neutral-500">
        Whole-custody ethnic composition
      </h3>
      <div class="chart mt-3" set:html={companion} />
      <p class="chart-download"><a href="/data/csv/remand-duration.csv" download>Download this chart's data (CSV)</a></p>
      <p class="mt-2 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed text-neutral-500">
        Share of the whole custody population, monthly:
        <span style="color:#534AB7; font-weight:600;">Black</span> and
        <span style="color:#D85A30; font-weight:600;">Mixed Heritage</span>
        children. This is the whole custody population, not the remand
        population; the Youth Custody Service does not publish remand by
        ethnicity monthly. A volume reduction that leaves these shares, the
        remand-stage Relative Rate Index, or the ethnic gap in remand duration
        unchanged is a volume success and a fairness failure.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed text-neutral-500">
        Sources: MoJ Youth Custody Service monthly youth custody report, June
        2026 edition (latest month provisional); YJS 2024-25.
        <a href={metricUrl} class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent">
          Read the full metric definition</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-neutral-500">
        Where to go next
      </h2>
      <ul class="mt-2">
        <li class="border-b border-neutral-200 py-4">
          <a
            href="/national"
            class="entry-link"
          >
            National picture
          </a>
          <span class="mt-1 block leading-relaxed">
            The road-to-remand cascade, and what it reveals about where
            disparity is generated.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="border-b border-neutral-200 py-4">
          <a
            href="/methods"
            class="entry-link"
          >
            Methods
          </a>
          <span class="mt-1 block leading-relaxed">
            Every source, every methodological decision, the disclosure
            controls, and how to reproduce the pipeline.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="border-b border-neutral-200 py-4">
          <a
            href="/about"
            class="entry-link"
          >
            About
          </a>
          <span class="mt-1 block leading-relaxed">
            What PRISM-R is for, who produces it, and its independence.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 font-sans text-sm text-neutral-500">
        <a
          href="/example-lambeth"
          class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent"
        >
          See worked example: Lambeth
        </a>
        <span class="block text-xs text-neutral-400">
          A preview of the local-level analysis the geographic explorer will
          provide.
        </span>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <style>
    .chart-download {
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
      font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;
      font-size: 0.72rem;
    }
    .chart-download a { color: #4a6b8c; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
    .masthead {
      border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e5e5;
    }
    /* The four headline figures, set in the display serif so the numerals
       carry the wordmark's character. */
    .stat-figure {
      font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
      font-weight: 500;
      font-size: 3.1rem;
      line-height: 1;
      letter-spacing: -0.02em;
      color: #1b3a5f;
      font-variant-numeric: lining-nums;
    }
    .entry-link {
      font-family: 'EB Garamond', Georgia, serif;
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      color: #1b3a5f;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
      text-decoration-color: #b9c4cf;
      transition: text-decoration-color 120ms ease;
    }
    .entry-link:hover {
      text-decoration-color: #1b3a5f;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
